# {{ product }} {{ version }}

Released {{ released }} by {{ author }}, dated {{ date }}.

## Front matter

Front matter is YAML between `---` lines at the very top of the file. These keys are reserved:

| Key            | Used for                                      |
| -------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| `yamd`         | The spec version the document targets         |
| `title`        | The document title                            |
| `author`       | One name or a list                            |
| `date`         | An ISO 8601 date                              |
| `lang`         | The language, for screen readers              |
| `vars`         | Values to insert with `{{ name }}`            |
| `bibliography` | A BibTeX or CSL-JSON file for citations       |
| `toc`          | `true` to add a table of contents             |

Any other key, such as `status` above, is kept for tools and queries.

## Variables

Insert values from `vars` with double braces:

- Product: {{ product }}
- Version: {{ version }}
- Storage per user: {{ limits.storage }}, uploads up to {{ limits.upload }}
- Docs: {{ links.docs }}

Built-in variables come from the reserved keys: {{ title }}, {{ author }}, {{ date }}.

Spaces inside the braces are optional: {{version}} and {{ version }} are the same.

## Where variables don't apply

- In code: `{{ version }}` stays as written.
- In code blocks:

  ```bash
  echo "{{ version }}"
  ```

- When escaped: \{{ version }}

## Upgrade note

:::note {title="Before you upgrade"}
Back up your data before upgrading. See {{ links.status }} for live status.
:::
